/-!
# Wythoff coarsening: the fan coarsening induced by folding data (#24)

## The referent, pinned before the code

Issue #24's item "Wythoff coarsening" is *not* a project coinage here: it is the standard **fan
coarsening induced by folding data**, and this file names it as such.

* A *fan* is `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.Fan`, CLS Definition 3.1.2, p. 106 (see
  `RationalPolyhedralCone.lean`); its cones are closed under faces and the intersection of two of
  its cones is a face of each.
* The *coarsening order* on fans is CLS §3.3: `Σ'` is a coarsening of `Σ` when every cone of `Σ`
  lies in a cone of `Σ'` (and the two have the same support). Only the cone-containment half is
  used here, because the support is untouched by the construction (see `Fan.isContainedIn_coarsen`
  and the remark on supports below).
* *Folding data* is the input supplied by #23's folding: a finite group `G` of `ℚ`-linear
  automorphisms of the ambient space, each induced by an endomorphism of the ambient lattice — for
  the ADE → BC foldings of the programme this `G` is generated by the involution that identifies
  the symmetric simple roots (Alexeev–Engel–Garza–Schaffler, *Compact moduli of Enriques surfaces
  with a numerical polarization of degree 2*, arXiv:2312.03638v2, §4.1 and §6; the ruling is
  recorded in this repository at `corpus/ambiguity-resolution-source-map-for-issues-1-and-4.md`,
  §I, "ADE->BC foldings via involutions").
* The **coarsening of a cone `C` by `G`** is the join of its orbit, `G · C := ⨆ g ∈ G, g '' C`
  (`FoldingData.orbitJoin`), and the **coarsening of a fan `Σ`** is the family
  `{G · C | C ∈ Σ}` (`FoldingData.coarsenedCones`). This is the coarsening whose cones are the
  joins of the orbits: it is the minimal family above `Σ` in the CLS §3.3 order that is stable
  under `G` (the stability is `coarsenedCones_stable`, proved below).

Two honesty notes on the citation. The page-level statement of the coarsening order inside CLS §3.3
is not verified in this environment; the section reference is the one the repository already uses
for fans (§3.1, Definition 3.1.2, p. 106, in `RationalPolyhedralCone.lean`). And the identification
of `G · C` with the chamber of the *folded* root system — that the orbit-join coarsening of the
Weyl fan of `A₃` is the Weyl fan of the folded diagram — is **not** proved here; what is proved is
the fan-theoretic coarsening itself and its two obligations.

## The two obligations of #24, as theorems and not as fields

The audit amendments require that equivariance and local finiteness be *proved theorems or layered
predicates, never bundled definitional fields*. Accordingly `FoldingData` carries only the group,
its finiteness and the lattice lifts (the folding input, not the coarsening's properties), and

* **equivariance** is `coarsenedCones_map_mem` / `coarsenedCones_stable`: the coarsened cone family
  is stable under the folding group, cone by cone;
* **local finiteness** is `coarsenedCones_locallyFinite`: the layered predicate
  `ConeFamilyLocallyFinite` (finitely many cones, and every point lies in finitely many of them),
  proved for the coarsened family.

## Main declarations

* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FoldingData`: the folding data of #23 as input.
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FoldingData.orbitJoin`: the coarsening `G · C` of a single cone.
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FoldingData.coarsenedCones`: the coarsened cone family of a fan.
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FoldingData.coarsenedCones_stable`: **equivariance**.
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FoldingData.coarsenedCones_locallyFinite`: **local finiteness**.
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.IsFanCoarsening`: the predicate under which the coarsened family
  is again a fan (salience of the orbit joins, and the two fan axioms the orbit joins must satisfy;
  each is a named hypothesis, not a field of a structure).
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.FoldingData.coarsenFan`: the coarsened fan.
* `LeanCategories.Lattices.Valued.Fan.fanCategory`, `FoldingData.coarsenFunctor`: **the coarsening
  is a functor** with named domain `CoarsenableFan F` and codomain `Fan i`, on the CLS §3.3
  coarsening order.

## References

* D. Cox, J. Little, S. Schenck, *Toric Varieties*, Graduate Studies in Mathematics 124, American
  Mathematical Society (2011), §3.1 (fans, Definition 3.1.2, p. 106) and §3.3 (the refinement and
  coarsening order on fans). Zotero item `UYAMB8YC`.
* V. Alexeev, P. Engel, D. Garza, A. Schaffler, *Compact moduli of Enriques surfaces with a
  numerical polarization of degree 2*, arXiv:2312.03638v2, §4.1 and §6 (ADE → BC foldings by
  involutions).

